>
> **Two — nonlinear attack signatures.** A zero-day exploit or a spoofed telemetry injection doesn't look like a linear deviation. It looks like a subtle, nonlinear twist in how features relate to each other — and even well-engineered classical features can't expose that twist.
>
> **Three — noise and adversarial perturbation.** In space, noise isn't an edge case, it's the operating environment — radiation-induced bit flips, jammers, deliberate data poisoning in these newer SAGIN networks. Classical models degrade sharply under exactly this kind of noise, and robustness here isn't optional, it's mission-critical.
>
> **Four — unseen attack geometries.** Classical RBF kernels have a *fixed* feature geometry, baked in at design time. They cannot adapt when adversaries evolve their tactics — and adversaries always evolve.
>
> Now here's the physics insight that motivated this whole project: **quantum feature maps embed data into an exponentially larger Hilbert space.** That space can expose correlations that are structurally invisible to a classical kernel — not because classical methods are badly tuned, but because the geometry they're searching in is fundamentally smaller."

