## SLIDE 5 — Our Hybrid Quantum–Classical Pipeline (5:50 – 9:00)

**This is the technical core. Slow, deliberate, walk the pipeline left to right exactly as it's drawn. This is also where you lose people if you rush — so don't.**

> "So here's what we actually built. Walk with me left to right.
>
> We start with raw network traffic — two datasets, CICIDS-2017 and an internal high-dimensional dataset we call SNT, 100,000 samples combined. I'll come back to why we chose two very different datasets in a minute, because that choice is actually central to our findings.
>
> That traffic goes through preprocessing — StandardScaler for the classical path, PCA down to 8 components where we test dimensionality reduction, and critically, we rescale everything into **[0, 2π]** — because that's the domain quantum rotation gates operate in. You can't feed a quantum circuit raw feature values; you have to encode them as rotation angles.
>
> That feeds into an **8-qubit quantum feature map** — and we test two versions. The **Fixed ZZ map** is parameter-free: a two-layer ring-topology entanglement structure, a fixed baseline with no learning involved. The **Trainable ZZ map** has **128 learnable parameters**, and this is our main contribution — it's optimized using something called **Kernel Target Alignment**, or KTA.
>
> Let me explain KTA in one sentence, because everything downstream depends on understanding it: **KTA measures how well your kernel's notion of 'similarity' matches the true class labels** — in plain terms, it asks 'are same-class points actually close together in this feature space, and are different-class points actually far apart?' Higher KTA means cleaner separation. And because our ZZ map is trainable, we can literally optimize the quantum circuit's parameters to *maximize* that alignment for each specific dataset — it's a feedback loop, shown right here on the slide: encode, measure KTA, adjust circuit parameters, repeat.
>
> The circuit outputs a **16-dimensional quantum feature vector** — expectation values of single qubits and correlated qubit pairs. That vector then becomes the *input* to completely standard classical classifiers — XGBoost, Random Forest, SVM. So to be clear: **we are not replacing classical ML. We're using quantum circuits as a feature engineering front-end** for classifiers people already trust and already deploy."

**Delivery note:** "We are not replacing classical ML, we're using quantum as a feature engineering front-end" — say this explicitly and clearly. It preempts the most common skeptical question in quantum ML talks ("so is this just quantum hype?") before anyone asks it.

